john lennon as gollum a steamy romance  between frodo and galadriel it all could  have happened but you'll never get to  see it j.r.r tolkien's vast and beloved  legendarium has been portrayed on screen  many times over the years long before  the first successful attempt came to  fruition however many others had tried  their hands at adapting middle earth for  the movies but what did tolkien himself  think about all this well in 1968 the  telegraph published an interview with  the author in which he expanded on his  views regarding the rights to adapt his  world for the screen as it turned out  tolkien had been solicited countless  times by people hoping to put the middle  earth stamp on everything from movies  and tv shows to puzzles and soaps but  his fans at the time weren't exactly  supportive of these ideas the article  cites one 17 year old fan who wrote to  tolkien don't let them make a movie out  of your ring it would be like putting  disneyland into the grand canyon for his  part it seems that tolkien agreed albeit  for slightly different reasons he told  the telegraph you can't cramp narrative  into dramatic form it would be easier to  film the odyssey much less happens in it  only a few storms  oh come to this happy place  welcome  nowadays it seems like nearly every  major movie and television franchise in  existence falls under the jurisdiction  of the house of mouse everything from  the marvel cinematic universe to the  star wars galaxy to the muppets is owned  and controlled by disney and there was a  very brief period in which the company  considered staking a claim in middle  earth in 1938 just a year after the  hobbit was released a disney animator  suggested combining elements of the  novel with richard wagner's ring cycle  of musical dramas for a segment in the  animated movie fantasia disney also  tried and failed to produce a film of  the hobbit during the 1950s disney  animator wolfgang rytherman once claimed  that disney even considered adapting the  lord of the rings but decided it was too  long and unwieldy for the big screen  this may have been for the best however  because tolkien truly detested disney in  one of his letters written in 1937 the  author claimed he loathed the studio and  insisted any illustrations for an  american publication of the hobbit not  be influenced by disney's animations  tolkien echoed these sentiments in a  later letter written in 1968 in which he  wrote i recognize disney's talent but it  has always seemed to me hopelessly  corrupted though in most of the pictures  proceeding from his studios there are  admirable or charming passages the  effect of all of them to me is  disgusting tolkien's first positive  response to an attempted hollywood  adaptation of the lord of the rings came  in 1957. that year american sci-fi  editor forrest j ackerman and producer  al brodak teamed up with writer morton  grady zimmerman to approach the author  with their idea for an animated  middle-earth movie tolkien was initially  impressed by their illustrations which  he noted to be in the style of  celebrated literary illustrator arthur  rackham he was less impressed by the  proposed storyline however which  featured what he called a general  degradation of the story nevertheless  the rights were optioned to team  ackerman partly because tolkien believed  he could acquire an author's veto on  anything he didn't like soon after  zimmerman sent tolkien a detailed  treatment of his lord of the rings story  tolkien's response written to ackerman  in a letter was anything but approving  mostly he complained about zimmerman's  rampant carelessness with the narrative  but tolkien's greatest criticism had to  do with the tone which he believed was  too action-heavy and failed to  accurately represent the heart of the  tale in the end the ackermann project  died a pitiful death the project was  shut down after failing to fund his  request for an extension to the rights  the whole world was gripped by  beatlemania in the 1960s perhaps  inevitably the runaway success of the  fab four's music was accompanied by a  number of live-action films including a  hard day's night and help it was during  this period that the beatles attempted  to get a lord of the rings movie off the  ground john lennon in particular was a  fan of tolkien and believed that he and  his band mates could very well star in  the picture lennon as gollum paul  mccartney as frodo george harrison as  gandalf and ringo starr as sam they even  approached stanley kubrick who had just  directed 1964's dr strangelove to helm  the project gentlemen you can't fight in  here this is the war room  sadly it wasn't to be for one thing  kubrick wasn't interested but it appears  that tolkien himself drove the final  nail into the beatles coffin in 2021  peter jackson told the bbc that the  author himself had refused the fab four  permission to make their movie jackson  claimed he didn't like the idea of a pop  group doing his story the director added  what would the beatles have done with  the lord of the rings soundtrack album  that would have been 14 or 15 beatles  songs that would have been pretty  incredible to listen to  in 1969 united artists acquired the film  rights to the lord of the rings tolkien  was 77 years old by that point and had  decided that he would not live to see  his novel appear on screen as such he  didn't particularly care who ended up  with the rights a few years later united  artists hired director john borman to  helm a live-action adaptation of the  novel porman would later become renowned  for movies such as deliverance and hope  and glory but at that time he had  directed just a handful of features  screenwriter rosbo pallenberg worked  with foreman to figure out how best to  bring middle earth to life on the silver  screen he ended up writing a 176 page  script for their movie the details of  the screenplay range from fascinating to  mortifying it featured a kabuki style  theatrical performance of middle-earth's  history a love scene for frodo and  galadriel and a battle of words fought  between gandalf and saruman foreman's  film was destined to fail however united  artists ran out of money after a number  of commercial setbacks finally deciding  not to move forward with the project the  good news though is that the script  still exists online just in case you  want to better imagine a movie in which  aragorn and boromir passionately kiss  beekeepers defend the walls of menace  tirith and mary blinds a horse with a  frying pan  the title of ralph bakshi's animated  middle-earth movie the lord of the rings  is something of a misnomer as it only  tells the story of the fellowship of the  ring and certain aspects of the two  towers gandalf  beyond all  hope  upon its release in 1978 the lord of the  rings was a considerable financial  success earning 30 million dollars  worldwide off an estimated 4 million  budget but it was met with poor reviews  from fans who were not pleased at all  with what they considered to be subpar  visuals and the fact that the movie  ended halfway through the story part 2  of bakshi's tale was green lit for  release in 1980. initially the film's  creators were optimistic but a sequel to  the lord of the rings simply never  materialized screenwriter peter s beagle  later recalled that it all came down to  creative differences between bakshi and  producer saul zance bakshi in particular  believed that the first movie simply  hadn't been as good as it could have  been in 1980 the production company rank  and bass which had made 1977's animated  hobbit movie released the return of the  king as an abc television special while  this had nothing to do with bakshi's  half-completed project the story does  pick up around the same point that the  lord of the rings ended providing fans  with a kind of unofficial conclusion to  the 1978 movie  in the early 1990s russian director  roman mitrofenov set out to bring an  animated adaptation of the hobbit to  life but the project fell apart when the  soviet union crumbled in 1991. here's  the fun part though the movie's six  minute prologue depicting the fall of  erebor and dale still exists online the  sequence opens on a party in dale in  which the townsfolk cavort and dance in  the square enter smaug who destroys dale  and takes erebor for his own as the snow  falls the scene cuts to a meeting of the  dwarves and gandalf who suggests they  hire a burglar to accompany them on  their quest for vengeance you'll  probably know all this of course if  you've already seen or read the hobbit  but the metrophenol prologue features a  couple of interesting quirks not seen in  the novel that peter jackson appears to  have referenced in his movies for  example the people of dale fly dragon  kites just like they do in an unexpected  journey and while the animated airborne  looks much different to jackson's  version the sight of dale covered by  snow will look all too familiar to fans  of the latter now it is a ruin  the desolation of smog  you can only imagine how the hobbit  movies might have looked if mitrofenov  had finished his adaptation  a 2021 essay written for polygon by film  critic drew mcweeney tells the story of  the war between jackson and disgraced  producer harvey weinstein over the lord  of the rings movies having worked with  jackson on many of his previous films  weinstein acquired the rights to the  lord of the rings from saul's hands and  hired the director and his screenwriting  partners bran walsh and philippa boyens  to make it a reality the first outline  jackson and walsh prepared for miramax  weinstein's company told a story across  two pictures the fellowship of the ring  and the war of the ring mcweeney writes  that the original scripts felt like  watching the final movies at 1.5 times  the speed many characters get little  development the shire scenes are short  and the elves are met only briefly  weinstein wasn't exactly cooperative  during pre-production and eventually  restricted jackson to a measly 75  million dollar budget so jackson and  walsh decided on a desperate gamble to  save their movie they leaked the script  for their project to mcweeney who then  reviewed it on anicool news weinstein  pushed jackson even further after that  demanding the lord of the rings become  one four hour film jackson refused and  weinstein conceded giving up the movie  on the condition that miramax be repaid  everything that had already been spent  on development luckily new line execs  bob shay and michael deluca rode in to  save the day committing to the project  and insisting on three lord of the rings  movies the rest as they say is history  in a hole in the ground  they lived  a hobbit  in 2008 j.r.r tolkien and guillermo del  toro fans alike were overjoyed to learn  that the acclaimed fantasy slash horror  director had been hired to helm new line  cinema's hobbit movies they were  somewhat less overjoyed to learn two  years later that del toro had left the  project citing production delays and  other commitments as the reasons for his  departure for better or for worse the  hobbit trilogy would eventually be  directed by peter jackson so how would  del toro's middle earth saga have played  out well for one thing it was intended  to take place over the course of two  movies rather than three an unexpected  journey and there and back again in 2011  new yorker writer daniel zalewski  visited del toro's home and got a  glimpse at how the films may have looked  he described seeing a sketch of smog the  dragon that resembled a flying axe and  drawings of other new monsters such as  an armor-plated troll that could curl up  into a ball del toro also elaborated on  making middle earth look like a  psychedelic painting rather than the  realistic new zealand landscapes that  peter jackson eventually utilized of  course it's impossible to say whether  del toro's hobbit movies would have been  any better or worse than jackson's but  one thing is for sure they clearly would  have made for one heck of a trip  [Music]

